WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:19.360
Ho Chi Minh, father of independent Vietnam,
was a man of contrasts.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.540
Standing at just four foot, eleven inches
and weighing less than a hundred pounds, he

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:25.700
appeared to pose no threat to anyone.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:30.570
Yet, through the force of his personality
and the steely determination of his will he

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:33.250
defeated two of the world’s mightiest nations.

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:37.890
He portrayed himself as a simple man of the
people yet he ruled over a repressive regime

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.409
that committed terrible atrocities.

00:00:39.409 --> 00:00:43.999
While many viewed him as the bringer of light
to a repressed people, to others he was simply

00:00:43.999 --> 00:00:46.519
a Communist spy planted by the Soviets.

00:00:46.519 --> 00:00:53.319
In today’s Biographics, we discover the
truth about the real Ho Chi Minh.

00:00:53.319 --> 00:01:00.300
Beginnings
Ho Chi Minh was born on May 19th, 1890 in

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.070
the village of Hoang Tru in what was French
Indochina.

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:06.720
He was given the birth name Nguyễn Sinh
Cung.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.680
From the age of five, he lived in the village
of Lang Sen in the Nghe An province.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.350
This was the hometown of his father, Nguyen
Sinh Sac.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:17.100
Sac was a man of learning, being a Confucian
scholar and a teacher.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.530
Cung was the third child, and the second son,
to Sac and his wife.

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:23.790
Another child would follow, but this son died
in infancy.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:27.780
By the end of the 19th Century, French Indochina
was France’s most prosperous colony, with

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:30.950
huge profits being made from rubber and rice
exports.

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:34.770
The jewel in the crown was the area known
today as Vietnam.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.780
The native people there mostly worked on the
large French owned rubber plantations.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:43.650
They were pressed onto work gangs to toil
for long hours and very little reward.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:46.420
Any rebellions were violently stamped out
by the French authorities.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:50.190
The Nguyen family were of the upper class
of Vietnamese society.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:54.690
Cung was instructed by his father until around
the age of seven years when formal instruction

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:57.510
began under a scholar by the name of Voung
Thuc Do.

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:01.000
The boy was a quick study, mastering the art
of Chinese calligraphy.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.910
Instruction in Confucianism lay at the core
of his studies and he rapidly came to embrace

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:05.910
its tenets.

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:10.319
Outside of the classroom, Cung displayed an
inquisitive mind and an adventurous spirit.

00:02:10.319 --> 00:02:12.340
His passions were kite flying and fishing.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:17.310
When he was ten years his father, in accordance
with Confucian tradition, gave him a new name

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:20.060
- Nguyễn Tất Thành, which means ‘ Nguyen
the Accomplished’.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:23.500
When he was eleven, Thanh’s family were
struck with tragedy.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:27.050
While his father was away on business, his
mother gave birth to a fourth child.

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:29.230
However, she did not survive the delivery.

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:30.280
Thanh was devastated.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:31.810
But he had no time to mourn.

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:35.450
He and his siblings had the responsibility
of looking after a newborn infant until their

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:36.580
father returned.

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:40.010
By this time, Sac had become a Mandarin, or
local official.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:42.940
The job kept him away from home for long periods
at a time.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:47.760
As a widower he now had to rely on family
and friends to help look after his children.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.069
In his pre-teens, Thanh began attending a
French boarding school in the city of Hue

00:02:51.069 --> 00:02:52.520
along with his older brother.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:55.870
Sac was of the view that, for his boys to
succeed in making their mark on the world,

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:58.590
they would need to have the benefit of a French
education.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:01.450
However, Sac was no supporter of the French
regime.

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:05.380
Despite being an official of the Indochinese
government, he was an outspoken critic of

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:09.340
the manner in which the local authorities
had so willingly ceded all power and control

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:10.340
to the French.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:12.460
This criticism did not go unnoticed.

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:16.410
Eventually he would be dismissed from his
position after a man died who Sac ordered

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:20.209
to be given 102 lashes as punishment for a
minor wrongdoing.

00:03:20.209 --> 00:03:23.920
Following his dismissal, Sac became greatly
embittered against the French Indochinese

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:24.920
bureaucracy.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.020
Unable to find work, he spent the rest of
his life travelling from village to village

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:29.819
in search of a way to make a living.

00:03:29.819 --> 00:03:32.260
Thanh saw very little of his father from then
on.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:36.640
He too became angry with the system, and especially
the French rulers who treated the local people

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:37.640
so badly.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:41.930
By 1908, Thanh had been studying at the National
Academy in Hue for four years.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:45.420
One day, he saw a large group of peasants
marching past the school.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:48.340
These people were protesting corruption in
the government and high taxation.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:53.959
It has long been related that Thanh joined
the protest and that he translated the demands

00:03:53.959 --> 00:03:58.540
of the people into French in order for the
colonial rulers to understand what their grievances

00:03:58.540 --> 00:03:59.540
were.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:01.560
As a result, the story goes, he was dismissed
from the school.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:05.599
However, a recently discovered French document
shows that he was admitted to the prestigious

00:04:05.599 --> 00:04:08.710
College Quoc Hoc in Hue in August, 1908.

00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:11.290
An expulsion would have made him ineligible
for entry.

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:15.879
In his later life, however, he pointed to
the protest in early 1908 as the point when

00:04:15.879 --> 00:04:18.320
his revolutionary fervor emerged.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:23.060
Upon completion of his studies in 1911, Thanh
took a position as a teacher at the Duc Thanh

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:26.190
school in Phan Tiet on the eastern coast.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:27.720
He stayed there for some six months.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:34.140
He then moved to Saigon, the bustling metropolis
in the southwestern part of the country.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:40.180
To France and Beyond
His four years of study had allowed Thanh

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:41.670
to become fluent in the French language.

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:45.270
It had also given him a good understanding
of French culture and history.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:49.720
He was aware of the principles of liberty,
equality and fraternity that had underpinned

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:50.800
the French revolution.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:55.310
He knew that those principles were not being
adopted in French Indochina but he was very

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:58.741
curious to see how they were being put into
action in France itself.

00:04:58.741 --> 00:05:03.729
In June of 1911, Thanh got his chance to satisfy
his urge to explore France.

00:05:03.729 --> 00:05:07.260
He managed to gain a position as a kitchen
helper on the French steamship Amirale de

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:08.260
Latouche-Tréville.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:11.260
A one-month journey brought the ship into
Marseilles on July 5th.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:15.160
On arrival in France, Thanh applied for entrance
into a school that trained officials to serve

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:16.260
in the colonies.

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.150
The application was denied.

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:21.020
Having arrived in France, he still needed
a means to support himself.

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:25.970
He decided that his best bet was to continue
working as a kitchen hand on ships, where

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:28.260
he knew he could secure work.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:32.830
As a result, he spent the next six years travelling
the world on various steamships.

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:36.919
This gave him the opportunity to visit many
colonial countries and compare the life and

00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:39.389
conditions to what he knew of back in French
Indochina.

00:05:39.389 --> 00:05:43.800
Thanh’s travels took him to Africa, South
America and the United States.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:47.810
On a 1912 trip, he jumped ship at Boston Harbor
and made his way to New York.

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:51.710
He claims to have lived and worked in Boston
and New York for some time, though the only

00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:55.720
evidence of his being in the U.S. at that
time is a letter he wrote that has a New York

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:56.720
postmark on it.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:00.680
While in the US, Thanh was surprised to learn
that immigrants to America had legal rights

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:04.550
that the French in Indochina would not even
give to the native people there.

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:08.509
Most importantly, they had the right to vote,
something which impressed him greatly.

00:06:08.509 --> 00:06:12.050
Just prior to the outbreak of World War One,
Thanh sailed for England.

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:14.550
He worked as a pastry chef at the Carlton
Hotel in Westminster.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:19.110
During his London years, he began to associate
with people of different nationalities, all

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:20.699
with one thing in common.

00:06:20.699 --> 00:06:24.990
They came from either British or French colonies
that were experiencing oppression and domination

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:26.259
by their colonial masters.

00:06:26.259 --> 00:06:31.150
Over the course of many late-night discussions,
Thanh came to dedicate himself to the liberation

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:32.949
of his homeland.

00:06:32.949 --> 00:06:40.340
Revolutionary
By 1917, Thanh had become an accomplished

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:43.479
pastry chef and had a solid career path laid
out before him.

00:06:43.479 --> 00:06:45.820
However, he decided to forego that opportunity.

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:49.229
He moved to Paris in order to learn the craft
of the revolutionary.

00:06:49.229 --> 00:06:53.100
He moved into the Parisian Vietnamese quarter,
which was quite large.

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:56.870
During his first twelve months in the French
capital he immersed himself in the culture

00:06:56.870 --> 00:06:58.310
and glamor of the city.

00:06:58.310 --> 00:07:01.530
To support himself, he worked as a cook and
a photo re-toucher.

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:05.981
But his primary purpose was to organize ex
pat Vietnamese into a force strong enough

00:07:05.981 --> 00:07:09.229
to effect some real change back in Indochina.

00:07:09.229 --> 00:07:13.199
Thanh was surprised to find that quite a number
of Frenchmen were sympathetic to the plight

00:07:13.199 --> 00:07:14.199
of the Vietnamese people.

00:07:14.199 --> 00:07:17.639
They encouraged him to publish his ideas about
what needed to be done.

00:07:17.639 --> 00:07:22.490
He did just that, producing an anti-colonial
newspaper targeted toward the expat-Vietnamese

00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:24.690
population throughout France.

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:27.100
Copies even made their way back to Indochina.

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:30.880
He wrote about the terrible conditions for
the native people, not just in France, but

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:33.419
in every colony, be it French or British.

00:07:33.419 --> 00:07:36.130
At this stage his writings did not call for
revolution.

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:40.170
He simply demanded fairer treatment and equal
rights.

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:43.630
The writings of Thanh made their way to the
Colonial Government in Indochina.

00:07:43.630 --> 00:07:48.110
He was immediately condemned as a revolutionary
and condemned to death if he ever returned

00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:49.110
to his homeland.

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:52.900
Meanwhile the native people of French Indochina
hailed him as a hero.

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:56.870
Thanh was in Paris at the time of the Treaty
of Versailles and became involved with a group

00:07:56.870 --> 00:08:00.980
of Vietnamese nationalists who tried to take
their message to the international congress.

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:05.640
The statesmen were basing their restructuring
of post-war Europe on the principals of self-determination

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:06.640
and democracy.

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:11.390
The Vietnamese nationalists wanted them to
apply those very principles to Vietnam.

00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:14.430
However, they were largely ignored by the
international congress.

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:23.500
Becoming Communist
With the establishment of Soviet Russia, the

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:29.210
worldwide socialist movement was suddenly
split between two ideologies; social democracy

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:30.210
and communism.

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:34.539
Thanh, living in Paris, made the fateful decision
to firmly throw in with the Communists.

00:08:34.539 --> 00:08:38.580
In 1920, he became one of the founding members
of the French Communist Party.

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:43.030
Thanh began to write pro-Communist articles
for the anti-colonial newspaper La Paria.

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:45.529
Then, three years later, he made a trip to
Russia.

00:08:45.529 --> 00:08:50.090
There he met with Marxist leaders who encouraged
him to view the struggle for Vietnamese independence

00:08:50.090 --> 00:08:54.029
as a part of the international Communist struggle
to overthrow Capitalism.

00:08:54.029 --> 00:08:58.850
He became a member of the Comintern, the organization
set up by the Soviets to take Communism to

00:08:58.850 --> 00:08:59.850
the world.

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:03.820
Thanh was convinced that the example set by
the Russian Communist revolution provided

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:06.850
the model for the overthrow of the French
in Indochina.

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:12.300
In June 1924, the Soviet government sent Thanh
to Canton, China to begin laying the groundwork

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:14.740
for the Communist take-over of Asia.

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:18.180
From this point on in his life, Thanh would
operate as an underground figure.

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:23.070
He travelled under aliases and lived in mountain
hideaways as he worked clandestinely to organize

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:25.560
Communist resistance movements.

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:28.110
In 1927, the Chinese authorities clamped down
on the Communists.

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:31.930
Thanh was nearly arrested, narrowly making
his escape into Siam.

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:34.460
In Siam, Thanh took on the persona of a Buddhist
monk.

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:36.050
This allowed him to travel freely.

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:38.560
In the north of the country there was a large
Vietnamese population.

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:42.630
It was there that he headed, preaching his
Communist message as he wandered from village

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:43.630
to village.

00:09:43.630 --> 00:09:46.560
Throughout the majority of the 1930’s, Thanh
disappeared from the record books.

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:51.250
There were rumours of him being in various
places, such as with Mao Tse Tung in the Chinese

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:54.090
highland or in Soviet Russia with Stalin.

00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:58.750
There were even reports that he was dead.

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:04.800
Ho Chi Minh
His re-emergence on the world stage just after

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:08.160
the outbreak of the Second World War proved
that last rumor wrong.

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:12.660
For the past fifteen years he had been biding
his time as he waited for the opportune moment

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:15.020
to lead his people in rebellion against the
French.

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:19.560
When France fell like a house of cards to
the German assault in just six weeks in mid-1940,

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:21.300
he felt that that time had come.

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:26.760
A new French government that cooperated with
the occupying Nazis was set up in Vichy, France.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:30.640
This Vichy government also took control of
French Indochina, but under the dominion of

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:35.570
the Japanese, who were one of the Axis Powers,
along with Germany and Italy.

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:40.160
At the beginning of 1941, Thanh re-entered
Indochina for the first time in thirty years.

00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:41.730
He entered the northern jungle.

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:45.240
He had great plans for an overthrow of the
French, but very little else.

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:48.480
He carried no weapons with him, had few followers
and little money.

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:52.230
He immediately set about recruiting support
among the mountain communities.

00:10:52.230 --> 00:10:56.950
His revolutionary group was called the Viet
Minh Doc Lap Minh, which was soon shortened

00:10:56.950 --> 00:10:57.970
to Viet Minh.

00:10:57.970 --> 00:11:02.649
The goal of this new group was to create a
broad-based nationalist movement for the reclaiming

00:11:02.649 --> 00:11:07.350
of Vietnamese independence and to gain the
support of other nations, especially the Chinese.

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:12.650
The Viet Minh also vowed to oust the ‘Japanese
fascists’, along with their ‘French accomplices.’

00:11:12.650 --> 00:11:17.020
It was at this time that Thanh adopted the
name by which he would become famous around

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:20.510
the world- Ho Chi Minh, which means ‘Bringer
of Light’.

00:11:20.510 --> 00:11:25.450
The name change was a calculated move to present
himself as a wise sage in the Confucian tradition.

00:11:25.450 --> 00:11:29.680
Ho lived the life of a peasant in order to
win the people to his cause.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:33.210
He spoke in simple language and rejected all
materialism in pursuit of the cause of nationalism.

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:38.149
He also cultivated the image of a lifelong
celibate who simply didn’t have time for

00:11:38.149 --> 00:11:39.149
a wife.

00:11:39.149 --> 00:11:42.130
In 1942 he went to China in order to recruit
extra manpower.

00:11:42.130 --> 00:11:46.620
However, he was arrested for being a Communist
and spent fourteen months in a Chinese prison.

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:49.870
He finally convinced his captors to release
him so that he could spy for them against

00:11:49.870 --> 00:11:51.350
the Japanese in Indochina.

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:53.630
He walked all the way back to his mountain
base.

00:11:53.630 --> 00:11:58.180
His followers had given him up for dead but
enthusiastically welcomed him back as their

00:11:58.180 --> 00:11:59.180
leader.

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:03.430
There were other revolutionary groups at the
time, but only the Viet Minh managed to establish

00:12:03.430 --> 00:12:05.970
a number of clandestine cells around the country.

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:09.910
Small scale guerrilla activities against the
Japanese continued throughout the duration

00:12:09.910 --> 00:12:10.910
of the war.

00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:13.050
Then the Japanese did something unexpected.

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:18.180
On March 9th, 1945, fearing an allied invasion,
they overthrew the French established government

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:21.370
and declared an end to the French colony of
Indochina.

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:26.519
The former nations of Laos and Cambodia were
freed from the colonial yolk to once again

00:12:26.519 --> 00:12:28.029
be independent kingdoms.

00:12:28.029 --> 00:12:32.680
The Vietnamese provinces of Annam and Tonkin
were given to the rulership of Bao Dai.

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:35.089
Still this was not genuine independence.

00:12:35.089 --> 00:12:36.530
The Viet Minh bided their time.

00:12:36.530 --> 00:12:39.912
They knew that the Japanese would eventually
be defeated and have to leave their land.

00:12:39.912 --> 00:12:43.440
At that time, they would step into the vacuum.

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:50.269
War with France
With the dropping of the two atomic bombs

00:12:50.269 --> 00:12:55.180
on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August, 1945,
the game was up for the Japanese.

00:12:55.180 --> 00:12:58.930
They swiftly left Indochina, creating the
vacuum that Ho Chi Minh had predicted.

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:03.450
Ho promptly declared Vietnam to be independent,
and set up a government, which was based at

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:04.450
Hanoi.

00:13:04.450 --> 00:13:07.570
The President who had been put in place by
the Japanese, Bao Dai, could see what the

00:13:07.570 --> 00:13:10.390
will of the people was and he quickly abdicated
his position.

00:13:10.390 --> 00:13:14.149
The French however, had absolutely no intentions
of giving up their hold on the colony.

00:13:14.149 --> 00:13:17.920
As far as they were concerned, now that the
war was over and the Axis powers had been

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:21.740
defeated, things would revert to the way they
were in the 1930’s.

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:24.579
But they were to find a greatly strengthened
Viet Minh.

00:13:24.579 --> 00:13:28.980
The American Office of Strategic Services
(OSS) had seen the Viet Minh as a useful ally

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:33.150
in defeating the Japanese and so had given
support to Ho Chi Minh’s guerrilla forces.

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:36.050
The new government set about working to bring
relief to the masses who had suffered under

00:13:36.050 --> 00:13:39.510
Japanese rule and were largely now at the
point of starvation.

00:13:39.510 --> 00:13:43.400
Ho put together a declaration of independence
which he modelled on the American version

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:44.870
of 1776.

00:13:44.870 --> 00:13:47.800
This was done in an attempt to win American
support.

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:50.240
For their part, the Americans were wary.

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:55.019
They had a natural inclination to want to
assist native peoples to overthrow their colonial

00:13:55.019 --> 00:13:57.760
overlords, just as they had done nearly two
hundred years before.

00:13:57.760 --> 00:14:01.560
But they also wanted to maintain as good a
relationship as possible with the French.

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:05.980
The two countries had a long history, dating
back to the very founding of the United States.

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:09.660
The French had backed them against the common
enemy, Great Britain, supplying millions of

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:11.930
dollars, ships and men.

00:14:11.930 --> 00:14:15.292
It was decided by the allies that the British
would occupy the area of the country south

00:14:15.292 --> 00:14:20.510
of the 16th parallel in order to accept the
surrender of the Japanese in Vietnam.

00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:24.260
The area north of the 16th Parallel would
be occupied by the Chinese.

00:14:24.260 --> 00:14:28.010
China agreed to allow the Viet Minh, under
Ho, to remain in power.

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:30.100
The British, though, saw it differently.

00:14:30.100 --> 00:14:33.649
They felt obligated to help the French restore
their pre-war position.

00:14:33.649 --> 00:14:38.350
In October, 1945, French forces, aided by
the British, landed in southern Vietnam and

00:14:38.350 --> 00:14:42.410
began to quash any and all Viet Minh resistance
that they encountered.

00:14:42.410 --> 00:14:46.250
The French status quo was established in Saigon
and other key centres.

00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:49.430
However, the countryside was rife with Viet
Minh guerrilla fighters.

00:14:49.430 --> 00:14:52.750
Then, under pressure from the allied powers,
the Chinese relented and allowed the French

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:56.110
to take power north of the French Parallel
also.

00:14:56.110 --> 00:14:58.959
The Viet Minh government, under Ho Chi Minh,
was forced to compromise.

00:14:58.959 --> 00:15:04.389
The French proposed a form of Vietnamese self-government
inside of a French ruled Indochina Federation.

00:15:04.389 --> 00:15:07.910
Ho and an entourage flew to Fontainebleau
in France to negotiate a peace deal.

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:09.279
But there was to be no deal.

00:15:09.279 --> 00:15:13.519
The French were only willing to concede limited
self-rule, which was a far cry from what the

00:15:13.519 --> 00:15:14.790
Viet Minh were demanding.

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:19.200
The talks broke down and Ho returned to Vietnam
without a solution.

00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:23.019
In November, 1946, the rising tension between
the Viet Minh and the French sparked into

00:15:23.019 --> 00:15:24.019
open warfare.

00:15:24.019 --> 00:15:27.600
The French began an air bombardment of the
coastal city of Haiphong.

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:30.160
This led to an evacuation of the port by the
Vietnamese.

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:34.449
Then, in December, the Viet Minh launched
a full-scale attack on the French position

00:15:34.449 --> 00:15:35.449
in Hanoi.

00:15:35.449 --> 00:15:38.639
When this failed to find success, the native
forces withdrew to the mountains in order

00:15:38.639 --> 00:15:42.600
to undertake a campaign of full-scale guerrilla
warfare.

00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:46.830
Over the next eight years, the Viet Minh,
under Ho’s skilful leadership ground down

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:48.430
the will of the French military.

00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:51.579
By 1954, the French were desperate to end
the war.

00:15:51.579 --> 00:15:54.990
They decided to launch a surprise attack on
the Viet Minh at a place called Dien Bien

00:15:54.990 --> 00:15:55.990
Phu.

00:15:55.990 --> 00:15:59.620
The French thought they were luring Ho’s
guerrillas into a trap but their plan backfired

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:03.529
terribly and Dien Bien Phu ended up as a disaster
for them.

00:16:03.529 --> 00:16:07.800
Over two months of fighting, at least fifteen
thousand French soldiers were killed.

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:10.589
The final Vietnamese assault was launched
on May 1st.

00:16:10.589 --> 00:16:14.930
For several days the battle raged on until
the French were finally subjugated.

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:17.790
More than 10,000 prisoners were taken.

00:16:17.790 --> 00:16:21.760
This was the final straw for the French and
they admitted defeat.

00:16:21.760 --> 00:16:29.920
Dividing Vietnam
In early 1954, the leaders of the United States,

00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:34.139
Britain, France and the Soviet Union agreed
to convene an international convention in

00:16:34.139 --> 00:16:40.290
Geneva in order to work out peace settlements
regarding the two major Asian conflicts – Korea

00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:41.290
and Indochina.

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:42.769
The Conference began in April.

00:16:42.769 --> 00:16:44.610
The first issue was Korea.

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:47.959
Discussions led nowhere and, in the end, the
terms of the ceasefire of the previous year

00:16:47.959 --> 00:16:50.199
continued to rule the situation.

00:16:50.199 --> 00:16:52.209
Then the focus switched to Indochina.

00:16:52.209 --> 00:16:56.519
After heated discussions, an agreement was
finally ironed out between the French and

00:16:56.519 --> 00:16:57.519
the Viet Minh.

00:16:57.519 --> 00:17:01.311
Cambodia and Laos were to be guaranteed their
independence, without the intervention or

00:17:01.311 --> 00:17:03.170
threat of Communist take-over.

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:07.809
In Vietnam itself, all Viet Minh forces were
to regroup north of th 17th Parallel.

00:17:07.809 --> 00:17:11.049
The French would withdraw to the south of
this arbitrary line.

00:17:11.049 --> 00:17:13.620
And so, the country was divided into North
and South.

00:17:13.620 --> 00:17:15.189
But only temporarily.

00:17:15.189 --> 00:17:20.419
The Geneva accord stipulated that elections
were to be held in two years to unify Vietnam

00:17:20.419 --> 00:17:22.889
under a single democratic government.

00:17:22.889 --> 00:17:26.879
A communist government under Ho Ci Minh was
now officially established north of the 17th

00:17:26.879 --> 00:17:27.879
Parallel.

00:17:27.879 --> 00:17:32.379
South of that line, the Republic of Vietnam
was installed with Ngo Dinh Diem as its first

00:17:32.379 --> 00:17:33.379
president.

00:17:33.379 --> 00:17:37.010
For 300 days after the signing of the Geneva
accords, the borders were open, allowing for

00:17:37.010 --> 00:17:39.109
people to move between north and south.

00:17:39.109 --> 00:17:43.429
Approximately one million people took advantage
of this opportunity to escape Communism and

00:17:43.429 --> 00:17:44.440
move south.

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:48.830
Ho now concentrated on building the Communist
state of North Vietnam.

00:17:48.830 --> 00:17:53.220
A land reform program was started based on
the model created by Mao Tse Tung in China.

00:17:53.220 --> 00:17:58.009
Many landowners were executed and corruption
was widespread.

00:17:58.009 --> 00:18:04.669
Another War
In the south, the US backed government of

00:18:04.669 --> 00:18:09.899
President Ngo Dinh Diem was anti-communist
and more corrupt that that in the north.

00:18:09.899 --> 00:18:12.330
Diem was unpopular with the people in the
south.

00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:16.700
He, along with his American backers did not
allow the Geneva accord stipulated unifying

00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:18.200
elections to take place.

00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:21.360
If they had, Ho Chi Minh would undoubtedly
have won.

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:25.639
By the end of the 1950’s anti-Diem sentiment
in the south had given rise to a civil war.

00:18:25.639 --> 00:18:27.999
The southern guerrillas were called Viet Cong.

00:18:27.999 --> 00:18:30.629
Ho sent Viet Minh fighters in to support them.

00:18:30.629 --> 00:18:34.850
In return, the US sent advisors in to help
the government forces of Diem.

00:18:34.850 --> 00:18:39.700
The real goal of the US, however, was to stop
the spread of Communism.

00:18:39.700 --> 00:18:47.779
The Bright Star Dims
By the mid 1960’s the Vietnam War was raging,

00:18:47.779 --> 00:18:50.340
with thousands of Americans fighting against
the Viet Minh.

00:18:50.340 --> 00:18:53.620
Ho Chi Minh was now a frail old man of 76
years.

00:18:53.620 --> 00:18:57.260
It is unlikely that he was involved any longer
in the day to day military operations.

00:18:57.260 --> 00:19:02.190
However, he was, as he had always been, the
major source of inspiration for the Vietnamese

00:19:02.190 --> 00:19:06.330
people to fight to win their independence
no matter the cost.

00:19:06.330 --> 00:19:09.100
Ho Chi Minh died on September 2, 1969.

00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:14.249
It would be six more years until the Americans
would be defeated and Vietnamese independence

00:19:14.249 --> 00:19:15.249
finally achieved.

00:19:15.249 --> 00:19:21.309
But the Vietnamese people owed that independence
to the tenacity, vision and determination

00:19:21.309 --> 00:19:22.980
of their visionary leader - Ho Chi Minh.

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:23.980
Sources:

00:19:23.980 --> 00:19:24.980
Pierre Brocheux: Ho Chi Minh: A Biography
William J. Duiker: Ho Chi Minh

00:19:24.980 --> 00:19:25.980
https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PvX_s732ZBM&amp;t=308s

00:19:25.980 --> 00:19:26.980
Ho Chi Minh
Ho Chi Minh, father of independent Vietnam,

00:19:26.980 --> 00:19:27.980
was a man of contrasts.

00:19:27.980 --> 00:19:28.980
Standing at just four foot, eleven inches
and weighing less than a hundred pounds, he

00:19:28.980 --> 00:19:29.980
appeared to pose no threat to anyone.

00:19:29.980 --> 00:19:30.980
Yet, through the force of his personality
and the steely determination of his will he

00:19:30.980 --> 00:19:31.980
defeated two of the wrold’s mightiest nations.

00:19:31.980 --> 00:19:32.980
He portrayed himself as a simple man of the
people yet he ruled over a repressive regime

00:19:32.980 --> 00:19:33.980
that committed terrible atrocities.

00:19:33.980 --> 00:19:34.980
While many viewed him as the bringer of light
to a repressed people, to others he was simply

00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:35.980
a Communist spy planted by the Soviets.

00:19:35.980 --> 00:19:36.980
In today’s Biographics, we discover the
truth about the real Ho Chi Minh.

00:19:36.980 --> 00:19:37.980
Beginnings
Ho Chi Minh was born on May 19th, 1890 in

00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:38.980
the village of Hoang Tru in what was French
Indochina.

00:19:38.980 --> 00:19:39.980
He was given the birth name Nguyễn Sinh
Cung.

00:19:39.980 --> 00:19:40.980
From the age of five, he lived in the village
of Lang Sen in the Nghe An province.

00:19:40.980 --> 00:19:41.980
This was the hometown of his father, Nguyen
Sinh Sac.

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:42.980
Sac was a man of learning, being a Confucian
scholar and a teacher.

00:19:42.980 --> 00:19:43.980
Cung was the third child, and the second son,
to Sac and his wife.

00:19:43.980 --> 00:19:44.980
Another child would follow, but this son died
in infancy.

00:19:44.980 --> 00:19:45.980
By the end of the 19th Century, French Indochina
was France’s most prosperous colony, with

00:19:45.980 --> 00:19:46.980
huge profits being made from rubber and rice
exports.

00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:47.980
The jewel in the crown was the area known
today as Vietnam.

00:19:47.980 --> 00:19:48.980
The native people there mostly worked on the
large French owned rubber plantations.

00:19:48.980 --> 00:19:49.980
They were pressed onto work gangs to toil
for long hours and very little reward.

00:19:49.980 --> 00:19:50.980
Any rebellions were violently stamped out
by the French authorities.

00:19:50.980 --> 00:19:51.980
The Nguyen family were of the upper class
of Vietnamese society.

00:19:51.980 --> 00:19:52.980
Cung was instructed by his father until around
the age of seven years when formal instruction

00:19:52.980 --> 00:19:53.980
began under a scholar by the name of Voung
Thuc Do.

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:54.980
The boy was a quick study, mastering the art
of Chinese calligraphy.

00:19:54.980 --> 00:19:55.980
Instruction in Confucianism lay at the core
of his studies and he rapidly came to embrace

00:19:55.980 --> 00:19:56.980
its tenets.

00:19:56.980 --> 00:19:57.980
Outside of the classroom, Cung displayed an
inquisitive mind and an adventurous spirit.

00:19:57.980 --> 00:19:58.980
His passions were kite flying and fishing.

00:19:58.980 --> 00:19:59.980
When he was ten years his father, in accordance
with Confucian tradition, gave him a new name

00:19:59.980 --> 00:20:00.980
- Nguyễn Tất Thành, which means ‘ Nguyen
the Accomplished’.

00:20:00.980 --> 00:20:01.980
When he was eleven, Thanh’s family were
struck with tragedy.

00:20:01.980 --> 00:20:02.980
While his father was away on business, his
mother gave birth to a fourth child.

00:20:02.980 --> 00:20:03.980
However, she did not survive the delivery.

00:20:03.980 --> 00:20:04.980
Thanh was devastated.

00:20:04.980 --> 00:20:05.980
But he had no time to mourn.

00:20:05.980 --> 00:20:06.980
He and his siblings had the responsibility
of looking after a newborn infant until their

00:20:06.980 --> 00:20:07.980
father returned.

00:20:07.980 --> 00:20:08.980
By this time, Sac had become a Mandarin, or
local official.

00:20:08.980 --> 00:20:09.980
The job kept him away from home for long periods
at a time.

00:20:09.980 --> 00:20:10.980
As a widower he now had to rely on family
and friends to help look after his children.

00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:11.980
In his pre-teens, Thanh began attending a
French boarding school in the city of Hue

00:20:11.980 --> 00:20:12.980
along with his older brother.

00:20:12.980 --> 00:20:13.980
Sac was of the view that, for his boys to
succeed in making their mark on the world,

00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:14.980
they would need to have the benefit of a French
education.

00:20:14.980 --> 00:20:15.980
However, Sac was no supporter of the French
regime.

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:16.980
Despite being an official of the Indochinese
government, he was an outspoken critic of

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:17.980
the manner in which the local authorities
had so willingly ceded all power and control

00:20:17.980 --> 00:20:18.980
to the French.

00:20:18.980 --> 00:20:19.980
This criticism did not go unnoticed.

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:20.980
Eventually he would be dismissed from his
position after a man died who Sac ordered

00:20:20.980 --> 00:20:21.980
to be given 102 lashes as punishment for a
minor wrongdoing.

00:20:21.980 --> 00:20:22.980
Following his dismissal, Sac became greatly
embittered against the French Indochinese

00:20:22.980 --> 00:20:23.980
bureaucracy.

00:20:23.980 --> 00:20:24.980
Unable to find work, he spent the rest of
his life travelling from village to village

00:20:24.980 --> 00:20:25.980
in search of a way to make a living.

00:20:25.980 --> 00:20:26.980
Thanh saw very little of his father from then
on.

00:20:26.980 --> 00:20:27.980
He too became angry with the system, and especially
the French rulers who treated the local people

00:20:27.980 --> 00:20:28.980
so badly.

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:29.980
By 1908, Thanh had been studying at the National
Academy in Hue for four years.

00:20:29.980 --> 00:20:30.980
One day, he saw a large group of peasants
marching past the school.

00:20:30.980 --> 00:20:31.980
These people were protesting corruption in
the government and high taxation.

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:32.980
It has long been related that Thanh joined
the protest and that he translated the demands

00:20:32.980 --> 00:20:33.980
of the people into French in order for the
colonial rulers to understand what their grievances

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:34.980
were.

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:35.980
As a result, the story goes, he was dismissed
from the school.

00:20:35.980 --> 00:20:36.980
However, a recently discovered French document
shows that he was admitted to the prestigious

00:20:36.980 --> 00:20:37.980
College Quoc Hoc in Hue in August, 1908.

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:38.980
An expulsion would have made him ineligible
for entry.

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:39.980
In his later life, however, he pointed to
the protest in early 1908 as the point when

00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:40.980
his revolutionary fervor emerged.

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:41.980
Upon completion of his studies in 1911, Thanh
took a position as a teacher at the Duc Thanh

00:20:41.980 --> 00:20:42.980
school in Phan Tiet on the eastern coast.

00:20:42.980 --> 00:20:43.980
He stayed there for some six months.

00:20:43.980 --> 00:20:44.980
He then moved to Saigon, the bustling metropolis
in the southwestern part of the country.

00:20:44.980 --> 00:20:45.980
To France and Beyond
His four years of study had allowed Thanh

00:20:45.980 --> 00:20:46.980
to become fluent in the French language.

00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:47.980
It had also given him a good understanding
of French culture and history.

00:20:47.980 --> 00:20:48.980
He was aware of the principles of liberty,
equality and fraternity that had underpinned

00:20:48.980 --> 00:20:49.980
the French revolution.

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:50.980
He knew that those principles were not being
adopted in French Indochina but he was very

00:20:50.980 --> 00:20:51.980
curious to see how they were being put into
action in France itself.

00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:52.980
In June of 1911, Thanh got his chance to satisfy
his urge to explore France.

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:53.980
He managed to gain a position as a kitchen
helper on the French steamship Amirale de

00:20:53.980 --> 00:20:54.980
Latouche-Tréville.

00:20:54.980 --> 00:20:55.980
A one-month journey brought the ship into
Marseilles on July 5th.

00:20:55.980 --> 00:20:56.980
On arrival in France, Thanh applied for entrance
into a school that trained officials to serve

00:20:56.980 --> 00:20:57.980
in the colonies.

00:20:57.980 --> 00:20:58.980
The application was denied.

00:20:58.980 --> 00:20:59.980
Having arrived in France, he still needed
a means to support himself.

00:20:59.980 --> 00:21:00.980
He decided that his best bet was to continue
working as a kitchen hand on ships, where

00:21:00.980 --> 00:21:01.980
he knew he could secure work.

00:21:01.980 --> 00:21:02.980
As a result, he spent the next six years travelling
the world on various steamships.

00:21:02.980 --> 00:21:03.980
This gave him the opportunity to visit many
colonial countries and compare the life and

00:21:03.980 --> 00:21:04.980
conditions to what he knew of back in French
Indochina.

00:21:04.980 --> 00:21:05.980
Thanh’s travels took him to Africa, South
America and the United States.

00:21:05.980 --> 00:21:06.980
On a 1912 trip, he jumped ship at Boston Harbor
and made his way to New York.

00:21:06.980 --> 00:21:07.980
He claims to have lived and worked in Boston
and New York for some time, though the only

00:21:07.980 --> 00:21:08.980
evidence of his being in the U.S. at that
time is a letter he wrote that has a New York

00:21:08.980 --> 00:21:09.980
postmark on it.

00:21:09.980 --> 00:21:10.980
While in the US, Thanh was surprised to learn
that immigrants to America had legal rights

00:21:10.980 --> 00:21:11.980
that the French in Indochina would not even
give to the native people there.

00:21:11.980 --> 00:21:12.980
Most importantly, they had the right to vote,
something which impressed him greatly.

00:21:12.980 --> 00:21:13.980
Just prior to the outbreak of World War One,
Thanh sailed for England.

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:14.980
He worked as a pastry chef at the Carlton
Hotel in Westminster.

00:21:14.980 --> 00:21:15.980
During his London years, he began to associate
with people of different nationalities, all

00:21:15.980 --> 00:21:16.980
with one thing in common.

00:21:16.980 --> 00:21:17.980
They came from either British or French colonies
that were experiencing oppression and domination

00:21:17.980 --> 00:21:18.980
by their colonial masters.

00:21:18.980 --> 00:21:19.980
Over the course of many late-night discussions,
Thanh came to dedicate himself to the liberation

00:21:19.980 --> 00:21:20.980
of his homeland.

00:21:20.980 --> 00:21:21.980
Revolutionary
By 1917, Thanh had become an accomplished

00:21:21.980 --> 00:21:22.980
pastry chef and had a solid carer path laid
out before him.

00:21:22.980 --> 00:21:23.980
However, he decided to forego that opportunity.

00:21:23.980 --> 00:21:24.980
He moved to Paris in order to learn the craft
of the revolutionary.

00:21:24.980 --> 00:21:25.980
He moved into the Parisian Vietnamese quarter,
which was quite large.

00:21:25.980 --> 00:21:26.980
During his first twelve months in the French
capital he immersed himself in the culture

00:21:26.980 --> 00:21:27.980
and glamor of the city.

00:21:27.980 --> 00:21:28.980
To support himself, he worked as a cook and
a photo re-toucher.

00:21:28.980 --> 00:21:29.980
But his primary purpose was to organize ex
pat Vietnamese into a force strong enough

00:21:29.980 --> 00:21:30.980
to effect some real change back in Indochina.

00:21:30.980 --> 00:21:31.980
Thanh was surprised to find that quite a number
of Frenchmen were sympathetic to the plight

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:32.980
of the Vietnamese people.

00:21:32.980 --> 00:21:33.980
They encouraged him to publish his ideas about
what needed to be done.

00:21:33.980 --> 00:21:34.980
He did just that, producing an anti-colonial
newspaper targeted toward the expat-Vietnamese

00:21:34.980 --> 00:21:35.980
population throughout France.

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:36.980
Copies even made their way back to Indochina.

00:21:36.980 --> 00:21:37.980
He wrote about the terrible conditions for
the native people, not just in France, but

00:21:37.980 --> 00:21:38.980
in every colony, be it French or British.

00:21:38.980 --> 00:21:39.980
At this stage his writings did not call for
revolution.

00:21:39.980 --> 00:21:40.980
He simply demanded fairer treatment and equal
rights.

00:21:40.980 --> 00:21:41.980
The writings of Thanh made their way to the
Colonial Government in Indochina.

00:21:41.980 --> 00:21:42.980
He was immediately condemned as a revolutionary
and condemned to death if he ever returned

00:21:42.980 --> 00:21:43.980
to his homeland.

00:21:43.980 --> 00:21:44.980
Meanwhile the native people of French Indochina
hailed him as a hero.

00:21:44.980 --> 00:21:45.980
Thanh was in Paris at the time of the Treaty
of Versailles and became involved with a group

00:21:45.980 --> 00:21:46.980
of Vietnamese nationalists who tried to take
their message to the international congress.

00:21:46.980 --> 00:21:47.980
The statesmen were basing their restructuring
of post-war Europe on the principals of self-determination

00:21:47.980 --> 00:21:48.980
and democracy.

00:21:48.980 --> 00:21:49.980
The Vietnamese nationalists wanted them to
apply those very principles to Vietnam.

00:21:49.980 --> 00:21:50.980
However, they were largely ignored by the
international congress.

00:21:50.980 --> 00:21:51.980
Becoming Communist
With the establishment of Soviet Russia, the

00:21:51.980 --> 00:21:52.980
worldwide socialist movement was suddenly
split between two ideologies; social democracy

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:53.980
and communism.

00:21:53.980 --> 00:21:54.980
Thanh, living in Paris, made the fateful decision
to firmly throw in with the Communists.

00:21:54.980 --> 00:21:55.980
In 1920, he became one of the founding members
of the French Communist Party.

00:21:55.980 --> 00:21:56.980
Thanh began to write pro-Communist articles
for the anti-colonial newspaper La Paria.

00:21:56.980 --> 00:21:57.980
Then, three years later, he made a trip to
Russia.

00:21:57.980 --> 00:21:58.980
There he met with Marxist leaders who encouraged
him to view the struggle for Vietnamese independence

00:21:58.980 --> 00:21:59.980
as a part of the international Communist struggle
to overthrow Capitalism.

00:21:59.980 --> 00:22:00.980
He became a member of the Comintern, the organization
set up by the Soviets to take Communism to

00:22:00.980 --> 00:22:01.980
the world.

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:02.980
Thanh was convinced that the example set by
the Russian Communist revolution provided

00:22:02.980 --> 00:22:03.980
the model for the overthrow of the French
in Indochina.

00:22:03.980 --> 00:22:04.980
In June 1924, the Soviet government sent Thanh
to Canton, China to begin laying the groundwork

00:22:04.980 --> 00:22:05.980
for the Communist take-over of Asia.

00:22:05.980 --> 00:22:06.980
From this point on in his life, Thanh would
operate as an underground figure.

00:22:06.980 --> 00:22:07.980
He travelled under aliases and lived in mountain
hideaways as he worked clandestinely to organize

00:22:07.980 --> 00:22:08.980
Communist resistance movements.

00:22:08.980 --> 00:22:09.980
In 1927, the Chinese authorities clamped down
on the Communists.

00:22:09.980 --> 00:22:10.980
Thanh was nearly arrested, narrowly making
his escape into Siam.

00:22:10.980 --> 00:22:11.980
In Siam, Thanh took on the persona of a Buddhist
monk.

00:22:11.980 --> 00:22:12.980
This allowed him to travel freely.

00:22:12.980 --> 00:22:13.980
In the north of the country there was a large
Vietnamese population.

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:14.980
It was there that he headed, preaching his
Communist message as he wandered from village

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:15.980
to village.

00:22:15.980 --> 00:22:16.980
Throughout the majority of the 1930’s, Thanh
disappeared from the record books.

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:17.980
There were rumours of him being in various
places, such as with Mao Tse Tung in the Chinese

00:22:17.980 --> 00:22:18.980
highland or in Soviet Russia with Stalin.

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:19.980
There were even reports that he was dead.

00:22:19.980 --> 00:22:20.980
Ho Chi Minh
His re-emergence on the world stage just after

00:22:20.980 --> 00:22:21.980
the outbreak of the Second World War proved
that last rumor wrong.

00:22:21.980 --> 00:22:22.980
For the past fifteen years he had been biding
his time as he waited for the opportune moment

00:22:22.980 --> 00:22:23.980
to lead his people in rebellion against the
French.

00:22:23.980 --> 00:22:24.980
When France fell like a house of cards to
the German assault in just six weeks in mid-1940,

00:22:24.980 --> 00:22:25.980
he felt that that time had come.

00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:26.980
A new French government that cooperated with
the occupying Nazis was set up in Vichy, France.

00:22:26.980 --> 00:22:27.980
This Vichy government also took control of
French Indochina, but under the dominion of

00:22:27.980 --> 00:22:28.980
the Japanese, who were one of the Axis Powers,
along with Germany and Italy.

00:22:28.980 --> 00:22:29.980
At the beginning of 1941, Thanh re-entered
Indochina for the first time in thirty years.

00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:30.980
He entered the northern jungle.

00:22:30.980 --> 00:22:31.980
He had great plans for an overthrow of the
French, but very little else.

00:22:31.980 --> 00:22:32.980
He carried no weapons with him, had few followers
and little money.

00:22:32.980 --> 00:22:33.980
He immediately set about recruiting support
among the mountain communities.

00:22:33.980 --> 00:22:34.980
His revolutionary group was called the Viet
Minh Doc Lap Minh, which was soon shortened

00:22:34.980 --> 00:22:35.980
to Viet Minh.

00:22:35.980 --> 00:22:36.980
The goal of this new group was to create a
broad-based nationalist movement for the reclaiming

00:22:36.980 --> 00:22:37.980
of Vietnamese independence and to gain the
support of other nations, especially the Chinese.

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:38.980
The Viet Minh also vowed to oust the ‘Japanese
fascists’, along with their ‘French accomplices.’

00:22:38.980 --> 00:22:39.980
It was at this time that Thanh adopted the
name by which he would become famous around

00:22:39.980 --> 00:22:40.980
the world- Ho Chi Minh, which means ‘Bring
of Light’.

00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:41.980
The name change was a calculated move to present
himself as a wise sage in the Confucian tradition.

00:22:41.980 --> 00:22:42.980
Ho lived the life of a peasant in order to
win the people to his cause.

00:22:42.980 --> 00:22:43.980
He spoke in simple language and rejected all
materialism in pursuit of the cause of nationalism.

00:22:43.980 --> 00:22:44.980
He also cultivated the image of a life-long
celibate who simply didn’t have time for

00:22:44.980 --> 00:22:45.980
a wife.

00:22:45.980 --> 00:22:46.980
In 1942 he went to China in order to recruit
extra manpower.

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:47.980
However, he was arrested for being a Communist
and spent fourteen months in a Chinese prison.

00:22:47.980 --> 00:22:48.980
He finally convinced his captors to release
him so that he could spy for them against

00:22:48.980 --> 00:22:49.980
the Japanese in Indochina.

00:22:49.980 --> 00:22:50.980
He walked all the way back to his mountain
base.

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:51.980
His followers had given him up for dead but
enthusiastically welcomed him back as their

00:22:51.980 --> 00:22:52.980
leader.

00:22:52.980 --> 00:22:53.980
There were other revolutionary groups at the
time, but only the Viet Minh managed to establish

00:22:53.980 --> 00:22:54.980
a number of clandestine cells around the country.

00:22:54.980 --> 00:22:55.980
Small scale guerrilla activities against the
Japanese continued throughout the duration

00:22:55.980 --> 00:22:56.980
of the war.

00:22:56.980 --> 00:22:57.980
Then the Japanese did something unexpected.

00:22:57.980 --> 00:22:58.980
On March 9th, 1945, fearing an allied invasion,
they overthrew the French established government

00:22:58.980 --> 00:22:59.980
and declared an end to the French colony of
Indochina.

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:00.980
The former natiions of Laos and Cambodia were
freed from the colonial yolk to once again

00:23:00.980 --> 00:23:01.980
be independent kingdoms.

00:23:01.980 --> 00:23:02.980
The Vietnamese provinces of Annam and Tonkin
were given to the rulership of Bao Dai.

00:23:02.980 --> 00:23:03.980
Still this was not genuine independence.

00:23:03.980 --> 00:23:04.980
The Viet Minh bided their time.

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:05.980
They knew that the Japanese would eventually
be defeated and have to leave their land.

00:23:05.980 --> 00:23:06.980
At that time, they would step into the vacuum.

00:23:06.980 --> 00:23:07.980
War with France
With the dropping of the two atomic bombs

00:23:07.980 --> 00:23:08.980
on Hiroshima and Nagasaki in August, 1945,
the game was up for the Japanese.

00:23:08.980 --> 00:23:09.980
They swiftly left Indochina, creating the
vacuum that Ho Chi Minh had predicted.

00:23:09.980 --> 00:23:10.980
Ho promptly declared Vietnam to be independent,
and set up a government, which was based at

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:11.980
Hanoi.

00:23:11.980 --> 00:23:12.980
The President who had been put in place by
the Japanese, Bao Dai, could see what the

00:23:12.980 --> 00:23:13.980
will of the people was and he quickly abdicated
his position.

00:23:13.980 --> 00:23:14.980
The French however, had absolutely no intentions
of giving up their hold on the colony.

00:23:14.980 --> 00:23:15.980
As far as they were concerned, now that the
war was over and the Axis powers had been

00:23:15.980 --> 00:23:16.980
defeated, things would revert to the way they
were in the 1930’s.

00:23:16.980 --> 00:23:17.980
But they were to find a greatly strengthened
Viet Minh.

00:23:17.980 --> 00:23:18.980
The American Office of Strategic Services
(OSS) had seen the Viet Minh as a useful ally

00:23:18.980 --> 00:23:19.980
in defeating the Japanese and so had given
support to Ho Chi Minh’s guerrilla forces.

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:20.980
The new government set about working to bring
relief to the masses who had suffered under

00:23:20.980 --> 00:23:21.980
Japanese rule and were largely now at the
point of starvation.

00:23:21.980 --> 00:23:22.980
Ho put together a declaration of independence
which he modelled on the American version

00:23:22.980 --> 00:23:23.980
of 1776.

00:23:23.980 --> 00:23:24.980
This was done in an attempt to win American
support.

00:23:24.980 --> 00:23:25.980
For their part, the Americans were wary.

00:23:25.980 --> 00:23:26.980
They had a natural inclination to want to
assist native peoples to overthrow their colonial

00:23:26.980 --> 00:23:27.980
overlords, just as they had done nearly two
hundred years before.

00:23:27.980 --> 00:23:28.980
But they also wanted to maintain as good a
relationship as possible with the French.

00:23:28.980 --> 00:23:29.980
The two countries had a long history, dating
back to the very founding of the United States.

00:23:29.980 --> 00:23:30.980
The French had backed them against the common
enemy, Great Britain, supplying millions of

00:23:30.980 --> 00:23:31.980
dollars, ships and men.

00:23:31.980 --> 00:23:32.980
It was decided by the allies that the British
would occupy the area of the country south

00:23:32.980 --> 00:23:33.980
of the 16th parallel in order to accept the
surrender of the Japanese in Vietnam.

00:23:33.980 --> 00:23:34.980
The area north of the 16th Parallel would
be occupied by the Chinese.

00:23:34.980 --> 00:23:35.980
China agreed to allow the Viet Minh, under
Ho, to remain in power.

00:23:35.980 --> 00:23:36.980
The British, though, saw it differently.

00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:37.980
They felt obligated to help the French restore
their pre-war position.

00:23:37.980 --> 00:23:38.980
In October, 1945, French forces, aided by
the British, landed in southern Vietnam and

00:23:38.980 --> 00:23:39.980
began to quash any and all Viet Minh resistance
that they encountered.

00:23:39.980 --> 00:23:40.980
The French status quo was established in Saigon
and other key centres.

00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:41.980
However, the countryside was rife with Viet
Minh guerrilla fighters.

00:23:41.980 --> 00:23:42.980
Then, under pressure from the allied powers,
the Chinese relented and allowed the French

00:23:42.980 --> 00:23:43.980
to take power north of the French Parallel
also.

00:23:43.980 --> 00:23:44.980
The Viet Minh government, under Ho Chi Minh,
was forced to compromise.

00:23:44.980 --> 00:23:45.980
The French proposed a form of Vietnamese self-government
inside of a French ruled Indochina Federation.

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:46.980
Ho and an entourage flew to Fontainebleau
in France to negotiate a peace deal.

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:47.980
But there was to be no deal.

00:23:47.980 --> 00:23:48.980
The French were only willing to concede limited
self-rule, which was a far cry from what the

00:23:48.980 --> 00:23:49.980
Viet Minh were demanding.

00:23:49.980 --> 00:23:50.980
The talks broke down and Ho returned to Vietnam
without a solution.

00:23:50.980 --> 00:23:51.980
In November, 1946, the rising tension between
the Viet Minh and the French sparked into

00:23:51.980 --> 00:23:52.980
open warfare.

00:23:52.980 --> 00:23:53.980
The French began an air bombardment of the
coastal city of Haiphong.

00:23:53.980 --> 00:23:54.980
This led to an evacuation of the port by the
Vietnamese.

00:23:54.980 --> 00:23:55.980
Then, in December, the Viet Minh launched
a full-scale attack on the French position

00:23:55.980 --> 00:23:56.980
in Hanoi.

00:23:56.980 --> 00:23:57.980
When this failed to find success, the native
forces withdrew to the mountains in order

00:23:57.980 --> 00:23:58.980
to undertake a campaign of full-scale guerrilla
warfare.

00:23:58.980 --> 00:23:59.980
Over the next eight years, the Viet Minh,
under Ho’s skilful leadership ground down

00:23:59.980 --> 00:24:00.980
the will of the French military.

00:24:00.980 --> 00:24:01.980
By 1954, the French were desperate to end
the war.

00:24:01.980 --> 00:24:02.980
They decided to launch a surprise attack on
the Viet Minh at a place called Dien Bien

00:24:02.980 --> 00:24:03.980
Phu.

00:24:03.980 --> 00:24:04.980
The French thought they were luring Ho’s
guerrillas into a trap but their plan backfired

00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:05.980
terribly and Dien Bien Phu ended up as a disaster
for them.

00:24:05.980 --> 00:24:06.980
Over two months of fighting, at least fifteen
thousand French soldiers were killed.

00:24:06.980 --> 00:24:07.980
The final Vietnamese assault was launched
on May 1st.

00:24:07.980 --> 00:24:08.980
For several days the battle raged on until
the French were finally subjugated.

00:24:08.980 --> 00:24:09.980
More than 10,000 prisoners were taken.

00:24:09.980 --> 00:24:10.980
This was the final straw for the French and
they admitted defeat.

00:24:10.980 --> 00:24:11.980
Dividing Vietnam
In early 1954, the leaders of the United States,

00:24:11.980 --> 00:24:12.980
Britain, France and the Soviet Union agreed
to convene an international convention in

00:24:12.980 --> 00:24:13.980
Geneva in order to work out peace settlements
regarding the two major Asian conflicts – Korea

00:24:13.980 --> 00:24:14.980
and Indochina.

00:24:14.980 --> 00:24:15.980
The Conference began in April.

00:24:15.980 --> 00:24:16.980
The first issue was Korea.

00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:17.980
Discussions led nowhere and, in the end, the
terms of the ceasefire of the previous year

00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:18.980
continued to rule the situation.

00:24:18.980 --> 00:24:19.980
Then the focus switched to Indochina.

00:24:19.980 --> 00:24:20.980
After heated discussions, an agreement was
finally ironed out between the French and

00:24:20.980 --> 00:24:21.980
the Viet Minh.

00:24:21.980 --> 00:24:22.980
Cambodia and Laos were to be guaranteed their
independence, without the intervention or

00:24:22.980 --> 00:24:23.980
threat of Communist take-over.

00:24:23.980 --> 00:24:24.980
In Vietnam itself, all Viet Minh forces were
to regroup north of th 17th Parallel.

00:24:24.980 --> 00:24:25.980
The French would withdraw to the south of
this arbitrary line.

00:24:25.980 --> 00:24:26.980
And so, the country was divided into North
and South.

00:24:26.980 --> 00:24:27.980
But only temporarily.

00:24:27.980 --> 00:24:28.980
The Geneva accord stipulated that elections
were to be held in two years to unify Vietnam

00:24:28.980 --> 00:24:29.980
under a single democratic government.

00:24:29.980 --> 00:24:30.980
A communist government under Ho Ci Minh was
now officially established north of the 17th

00:24:30.980 --> 00:24:31.980
Parallel.

00:24:31.980 --> 00:24:32.980
South of that line, the Republic of Vietnam
was installed with Ngo Dinh Diem as its first

00:24:32.980 --> 00:24:33.980
president.

00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:34.980
For 300 days after the signing of the Geneva
accords, the borders were open, allowing for

00:24:34.980 --> 00:24:35.980
people to move between north and south.

00:24:35.980 --> 00:24:36.980
Approximately one million people took advantage
of this opportunity to escape Communism and

00:24:36.980 --> 00:24:37.980
move south.

00:24:37.980 --> 00:24:38.980
Ho now concentrated on building the Communist
state of North Vietnam.

00:24:38.980 --> 00:24:39.980
A land reform program was started based on
the model created by Mao Tse Tung in China.

00:24:39.980 --> 00:24:40.980
Many landowners were executed and corruption
was widespread.

00:24:40.980 --> 00:24:41.980
Another War
In the south, the US backed government of

00:24:41.980 --> 00:24:42.980
President Ngo Dinh Diem was anti-communist
and more corrupt that that in the north.

00:24:42.980 --> 00:24:43.980
Diem was unpopular with the people in the
south.

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:44.980
He, along with his American backers did not
allow the Geneva accord stipulated unifying

00:24:44.980 --> 00:24:45.980
elections to take place.

00:24:45.980 --> 00:24:46.980
If they had, Ho Chi Minh would undoubtedly
have won.

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:47.980
By the end of the 1950’s anti-Diem sentiment
in the south had given rise to a civil war.

00:24:47.980 --> 00:24:48.980
The southern guerrillas were called Viet Cong.

00:24:48.980 --> 00:24:49.980
Ho sent Viet Minh fighters in to support them.

00:24:49.980 --> 00:24:50.980
In return, the US sent advisors in to help
the government forces of Diem.

00:24:50.980 --> 00:24:51.980
The real goal of the US, however, was to stop
the spread of Communism.

00:24:51.980 --> 00:24:52.980
The Bright Star Dims
By the mid 1960’s the Vietnam War was raging,

00:24:52.980 --> 00:24:53.980
with thousands of Americans fighting against
the Viet Minh.

00:24:53.980 --> 00:24:54.980
Ho Chi Minh was now a frail old man of 76
years.

00:24:54.980 --> 00:24:55.980
It is unlikely that he was involved any longer
in the day to day military operations.

00:24:55.980 --> 00:24:56.980
However, he was, as he had always been, the
major source of inspiration for the Vietnamese

00:24:56.980 --> 00:24:57.980
people to fight to win their independence
no matter the cost.

00:24:57.980 --> 00:24:58.980
Ho Chi Minh died on September 2, 1969.

00:24:58.980 --> 00:24:59.980
It would be six more years until the Americans
would be defeated and Vietnamese independence

00:24:59.980 --> 00:25:00.980
finally achieved.

00:25:00.980 --> 00:25:01.980
But the Vietnamese people owed that independence
to the tenacity, vision and determination

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:01.987
of their visionary leader - Ho Chi Minh.

